<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        EOP-163. Makes the rollback of the 2026-08-22 join code widening changelog refuse
        instead of destroying live join codes.

        A note on style before anything else: this header cannot name the file it protects,
        because that filename contains a double hyphen and XML forbids a double hyphen inside
        a comment. The rollback comment below is a Liquibase <comment> element rather than an
        XML comment, so it can and does name it in full.

        This changeset adds no schema. It exists only so that its own rollback runs, and
        Liquibase unwinds changesets in reverse execution order, so a guard placed here
        executes before the two rollbacks it protects. That is the only lever available: a
        merged changeset is immutable (id plus author plus filepath is its identity), so the
        widening's own rollbacks cannot be edited to add the guard in place.

        WHAT WAS WRONG

        The widening rolls back in two steps, in this order:

          1. its changeset 002 rollback:  UPDATE game_session
                                          SET join_code = SUBSTRING(join_code, 1, 6)
                                          WHERE LENGTH(join_code) = 8 AND join_code LIKE '%00'
          2. its changeset 001 rollback:  narrow join_code from VARCHAR(8) back to VARCHAR(6)

        Both steps lose data once real eight character codes exist, and they lose it
        differently on each engine. Measured on PostgreSQL 17 and H2 2.4 under EOP-164 and
        EOP-163:

          * PostgreSQL, step 2. Liquibase narrows with an explicit cast, and an explicit cast
            to varchar(n) truncates in PostgreSQL rather than raising "value too long". So
            every live eight character code is silently rewritten to its first six characters
            and Liquibase reports "Rollback command completed successfully." Silent data loss
            reported as success is the primary defect, and it is not confined to codes ending
            in two zeros.
          * PostgreSQL, step 2. That truncation can also collide two sessions whose codes
            share a six character prefix on uq_game_session_join_code, aborting the rollback.
            The abort is data dependent, so a rehearsal against an empty database passes and
            the same rollback fails against production.
          * H2, step 2. Narrowing hard fails with "Value too long for column" instead of
            truncating, which is louder but is still an aborted rollback.
          * H2, step 1. H2 is not safe either, because step 1 runs first and silently
            truncates a genuine code ending in two zeros. On H2 that rollback then completes,
            so the outcome for such a code is the same clean looking report and destroyed
            data that PostgreSQL produces for every code.

        WHY REFUSING IS THE FIX RATHER THAN A BETTER PREDICATE

        Tightening step 1's LIKE predicate cannot help. After the forward migration a padded
        code (QRSTUV becomes QRSTUV00) is byte for byte indistinguishable from a genuinely
        generated code that happens to end in two zeros. The forward migration destroyed the
        distinction, and nothing in the schema records which rows it touched. So no rollback
        can be lossless on populated data. The honest behaviour is to refuse before anything
        is modified, and to recover by rolling forward.

        HOW THE GUARD WORKS

        The rollback adds a CHECK constraint asserting that every join code still fits in
        VARCHAR(6), then immediately drops it again. Adding a validated CHECK constraint
        fails if and only if a violating row exists, so:

          * a join code too wide to survive the narrowing gives a failed ALTER TABLE, the
            whole rollback aborts, and no row and no column type has been touched;
          * no such code (an empty database, or one holding only six character codes) gives
            two successful statements, the schema is left exactly as it was, and the widening
            unwinds losslessly as before.

        The guard is deliberately plain ANSI DDL rather than a PostgreSQL DO block with
        RAISE EXCEPTION, a Liquibase stop change, or a Java customChange:

          * preConditions is not a legal child of rollback in the Liquibase schema, and a
            changeSet level precondition is evaluated on forward update too, where the
            widening's changeset 002 deliberately creates the very eight character codes it
            would assert against. So the obvious mechanism does not exist.
          * the stop change throws unconditionally, which would also refuse the two rollbacks
            that are legitimately lossless: an empty database and a six character only one.
          * a DO block with RAISE EXCEPTION gives a friendlier message but has no H2
            equivalent, and as the H2 findings above show the guard is needed on both engines.
          * a customChange would work on both engines but would make the rollback depend on
            application classes being on the classpath, which is exactly what a DBA unwinding
            with the Liquibase CLI does not have.

        OPERATIONAL NOTES

        This changeset executes after the widening, so it is unwound before it. Fully
        unwinding the widening therefore needs rollbackCount=3, not 2.

        The guard only runs first because includeAll orders changes/ alphabetically and this
        filename sorts after the widening's. A new changelog dated before 2026-08-23 dropped
        into changes/ would sort between the two and silently defeat the guard. Nothing in
        the build detects that, so it is a review concern.

        Refusing is conservative in one respect worth recording: a database whose only wide
        codes are ones this migration itself padded is in fact losslessly reversible, and the
        guard refuses it anyway, because as explained above nothing can tell those rows from
        genuine ones. Padded sessions are bounded by the 24 hour expires_at TTL added in
        006-session-expiry.xml, so that window closes on its own.

        Changesets are immutable once merged. Later stories add files.
    -->

    <changeSet id="001" author="miguel">
        <comment>
            Marker changeset for EOP-163. Applies no schema change; exists so that its rollback can
            refuse to unwind the join code widening when doing so would destroy live join codes.
        </comment>

        <empty/>

        <rollback>
            <comment>
                Rolling back 2026-08-22--widen-join-code-to-8-characters.xml is NOT reversible on
                populated data, and this guard refuses rather than pretending otherwise. Once that
                forward migration has run, a padded code (QRSTUV becomes QRSTUV00) is byte for byte
                indistinguishable from a genuinely generated eight character code, so narrowing the
                column back either truncates real join codes, silently on PostgreSQL, or aborts on
                uq_game_session_join_code. Recovery from a bad widening is a forward migration, not
                a rollback. The two statements below add a CHECK constraint and immediately drop it
                again: adding it fails if any join code is too wide to survive the narrowing, which
                aborts the rollback before that changelog's changeset 002 UPDATE or its changeset
                001 narrowing can modify anything; if every code still fits, both succeed and leave
                the schema untouched.
            </comment>

            <sql>
                ALTER TABLE game_session
                    ADD CONSTRAINT ck_eop163_join_code_fits_varchar6
                    CHECK (LENGTH(join_code) &lt;= 6)
            </sql>

            <sql>
                ALTER TABLE game_session
                    DROP CONSTRAINT ck_eop163_join_code_fits_varchar6
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
